<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hari Singh Nalwa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1791">1791</a>–<a href="/years/1837">1837</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hari Singh Nalwa was a commander of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Maharaja Ranjit Singh</a>. He took part in the conquests of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kasur">Kasur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/multan">Multan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshawar">Peshawar</a>, and served as governor of Kashmir and later of the Peshawar frontier, where he built forts against Afghan attacks. He was mortally wounded at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-jamrud">Battle of Jamrud</a> in 1837 and is remembered as one of the greatest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> generals.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hari-singh-nalwa.jpg" alt="Hari Singh Nalwa" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hasan al-Din · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Painting_of_Hari_Singh_Nalwa,_by_Hasan_al-Din,_Lahore,_ca.1845-50.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hari Singh was born on 29 April 1791 in Gujranwala, in the Majha region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, to Dharam Kaur and Gurdial Singh of the Uppal Khatri community. His father died in 1798, and at ten he was initiated as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalsa">Khalsa</a>. In 1804 his mother sent him to the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a> over a property dispute; impressed by his family's service to the maharaja's ancestors and by his skill as a horseman and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">musketeer</a>, Ranjit Singh decided in his favour and made him a personal attendant. That year, by tradition, he killed a tiger with a dagger, earning the name Baghmar, Tiger-killer.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Commissioned a sardar in 1804, he fought at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kasur">Kasur</a> in 1807 aged sixteen and won his first independent command at Sialkot. He was badly wounded by a fire-pot while climbing the walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/multan">Multan</a> in 1810, fought at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attock">Attock</a> in 1813, and was chiefly instrumental in taking the citadel of Multan in 1818, a siege in which 1,900 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a> were killed. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> expedition of 1819 he routed the army of Raja Agarullah Khan of Rajouri, and at Mangal in 1821 his 7,000 men defeated a tribal force reported at 25,000 to 30,000.</p><p>His administration covered a third of the empire: he was governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> in 1820–21, of Greater Hazara from 1822 to 1837 and twice of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshawar">Peshawar</a>, and he set up a mint for revenue collection. In 1831 he led a diplomatic mission to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-william-bentinck">Lord William Bentinck</a>, Governor-General of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>, and voiced strong reservations about having <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharak-singh">Kharak Singh</a> acknowledged as heir. At least 56 buildings are attributed to him, among them the planned fortified town of Haripur, built in 1822.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1837, while troops were away for the wedding of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nau-nihal-singh">Nau Nihal Singh</a>, Afghan forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dost-mohammad-khan">Dost Mohammad Khan</a> surrounded his lieutenant Mahan Singh and 600 men at Jamrud. Riding to their rescue, Hari Singh was mortally wounded; by Hari Ram Gupta's account he was struck by two bullets, while the Afghan chronicle Siraj al-Tawarikh says he fell in a duel with Wazir Akbar Khan. He asked that his death be kept secret until reinforcements came, and he was cremated in the fort. Martial ballads, a 1967 film song and a postage stamp of 2013 recall him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hari Singh Nalwa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>That frontier was defended in 1837 at the Battle of Jamrud, where the general <b>Hari Singh Nalwa</b> was killed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Gujranwala, in what is now <a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=32.15,74.18&amp;year=1820" aria-label="Gujranwala on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6458 994 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Hari Singh Nalwa"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6908 1264h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6908 1264h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=32.15,74.18&amp;year=1820">What was here in 1820? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
